TODO
====

- sig-event-loop-integration: `lib/sig` has no self-pipe/`signalfd` support,
  so a handler installed via `sig_catch`/`sig_action` has to do its work (or
  hand off some other way) from signal-handler context; there's no built-in
  way to turn a caught signal into something a `select`/`poll`/`epoll` loop
  can wait on. Noted while making the module more generally reusable
  (see `doc/sig.md`) -- this is usually the actual reason a server-oriented
  program reaches for a signal library.

- exec-intercept-system-signal-semantics: `exec-intercept.c`'s `system()`/
  `popen()` overrides (used when the map redirects `sh`/`/bin/sh` to another
  shell) reimplement the fork+exec+wait sequence directly instead of calling
  the libc functions, so they skip the POSIX-mandated signal handling around
  the child (`SIGINT`/`SIGQUIT` ignored and `SIGCHLD` blocked in the parent
  for the duration of the call). Fine for the compatibility-testing use case
  this tool targets, but a caller relying on that signal behavior around
  `system()`/`popen()` would see a difference when the shell is redirected.

- genmakefile-create-module-gcc-only: `--create-module` (loadable
  `-shared`/`-fPIC` modules, e.g. QuickJS native modules) only sets
  `commands.link_module` in the gcc/clang branch of `set_compiler_type()`
  (`genmakefile.c`, alongside `commands.link`). Every other compiler
  branch (msvc, borland, sdcc, xc8, ...) leaves it empty, so
  `--create-module` with `-t msvc` etc. silently generates a module rule
  with an empty recipe instead of failing loudly. Only gcc-family
  toolchains make sense as loadable-module builders in practice, but the
  silent-empty-recipe failure mode for the rest is worth a guard/warning
  (mirroring the existing sdcc `--create-libs` warning) rather than a
  quiet no-op Makefile rule.

- genmakefile-cross-compile-flag-disabled: the `if(cygming && 0) { ... }`
  block in `set_compiler_type()`'s trailer (`genmakefile.c`, right after
  the compiler-branch chain) that would set `CROSS_COMPILE` from
  `tools.toolchain` (e.g. `x86_64-w64-mingw32-`) is permanently disabled
  by the `&& 0`. In practice this doesn't block cross-compiling -- the
  separate `-c/--cross PREFIX` flag (`cross_compile` global, applied a
  few lines below via `if(*cross_compile) { var_set("CROSS_COMPILE",
  cross_compile); ... }`) already does the same job when passed
  explicitly -- but `-t mingw64` alone (without also passing `-c
  x86_64-w64-mingw32-`) silently produces a plain, non-prefixed `CC =
  gcc` instead of a real cross compiler. Worth either wiring up the
  disabled block (auto-deriving the prefix from `tools.toolchain`) or
  documenting in `--help` that `-c` is required alongside `-t
  mingw64`/`msys`/`cygwin`.

- input-c-prefix-detection-more-coverage: `src/genmakefile/input.c`'s
  decoration-prefix detection (`input_word_is_command()`,
  `input_find_command_start()`, `input_build_prefix_pattern()`,
  `input_match_prefix_pattern()`, `input_line_classify()`,
  `input_detect_prefix_pattern()`, wired into `input_process_file()`)
  lets a `--infile`'s lines carry an arbitrary decoration prefix (shell
  trace markers, logging-shim banners, ...) in front of the real
  command, detecting the decoration's shape from the file's own content
  instead of hardcoding one. Committed after smoke-testing four
  one-rule (`gcc -c ...` + `gcc -o ...`) `--infile` samples --
  undecorated, a `[timestamp] pidNNNN:`-style prefix, a shell
  `set -x`-style `+ ` prefix, and one deliberately ending in a lone
  trailing `\` with no newline -- all producing identical generated
  rules with no hang under a 2GB `ulimit -v`.

  Still worth covering: messier real-world decoration (multi-line/
  wrapped log entries, decoration containing characters that look like
  flags or paths, a file with no compile+link pair at all so
  `input_detect_prefix_pattern()` never confirms a pattern) and
  whether `input_word_is_command()`'s `is_command_b()` calls (one per
  token, on every line) are a performance concern on large
  `--infile`s.
